    passage: "Passage A\nThe concept of a universal basic income (UBI)—a regular cash payment to all citizens regardless of employment status—has gained traction as a response to automation and job displacement. Pilot programs around the world have shown promising results: recipients report improved mental health, greater investment in education and training, and increased entrepreneurial activity. UBI also eliminates the bureaucratic overhead of means-tested welfare programs, potentially reducing administrative costs.\n\nPassage B\nUniversal basic income faces significant practical and philosophical challenges. The cost of providing a meaningful UBI to an entire population would be enormous, requiring either substantial tax increases or cuts to other essential services. Furthermore, removing the link between work and income could reduce labor force participation at a time when many economies face labor shortages. Critics also argue that UBI fails to address the root causes of poverty, such as inadequate housing, healthcare, and education, which require targeted interventions rather than a one-size-fits-all cash payment.",
