    passage: "Passage A\nThe increasing use of body cameras by police officers has been promoted as a tool for increasing transparency and accountability. Studies suggest that when officers wear cameras, both complaints against officers and use-of-force incidents decrease. The presence of cameras encourages all parties to behave more lawfully, and the footage provides an objective record that can resolve disputes about what occurred during an encounter.\n\nPassage B\nThe effectiveness of body cameras depends heavily on how they are implemented. When officers have discretion over when to activate their cameras, the accountability benefits diminish significantly. Furthermore, the existence of video footage does not always lead to clearer outcomes—different viewers often interpret the same footage differently based on their preexisting biases. Concerns about privacy, particularly for victims and witnesses, also complicate the widespread adoption of body cameras without clear policies governing footage access and retention.",
