    passage: "Passage A\nThe decline of honeybee populations worldwide poses a serious threat to global agriculture. Approximately one-third of the food we consume depends on insect pollination, and honeybees are the most economically valuable pollinators. The phenomenon known as colony collapse disorder, along with pesticide exposure, habitat loss, and climate change, has contributed to significant losses in bee colonies. Without immediate action to protect bee populations, we risk severe disruptions to food production systems.\n\nPassage B\nWhile honeybee declines are concerning, the focus on honeybees has diverted attention from the hundreds of other native bee species that are also essential pollinators. Many native bees are actually more effective pollinators than honeybees for certain crops, yet they receive far less research funding and conservation attention. Moreover, the honeybee is a domesticated, non-native species in many regions, and its success can come at the expense of native pollinators. A truly effective conservation strategy must address the diversity of pollinators, not just the most visible one.",
