    passage: "Passage A\nMerit-based pay for teachers, where salary increases are tied to student test scores or performance evaluations, is a policy that has been championed by education reformers. Proponents argue that linking compensation to performance incentivizes excellence, attracts high-achieving individuals to the profession, and rewards the most effective educators. In theory, this approach would improve student outcomes by motivating teachers to refine their practice and by retaining the best teachers in the classroom.\n\nPassage B\nThe assumptions underlying merit-based pay for teachers rest on a flawed understanding of what motivates effective teaching. Research in psychology suggests that financial incentives can actually undermine intrinsic motivation—the passion for teaching and commitment to student development that draws many people to the profession. When teachers are motivated primarily by measurable outcomes, they may focus on test preparation at the expense of deeper learning. Furthermore, measuring teacher effectiveness is inherently difficult: student test scores reflect many factors outside a teacher's control, including poverty, family support, and prior preparation. Performance evaluations, meanwhile, can be subjective and inconsistent. Merit pay systems, therefore, risk demoralizing teachers rather than motivating them.",
